ght amber tint. The brown color from that fraction of a bean just can’t compete. If you’ve ever made vanilla ice cream from scratch with a steeped bean pod, you might see it turn a pale cream or beige, but never brown. ### 4. Commercial extracts often use even less bean per volume Commercial vanilla extract is standardized by the FDA: single-fold extract must contain the extractive matter from at least 13.35 ounces of vanilla beans per gallon of liquid. That’s roughly equivalent to 6–8 beans per cup, similar to homemade ratios. But large-scale producers often use more efficient extraction methods (like percolation or counter-current extraction) to get more flavor out of fewer beans, and they may blend extracts from different bean grades. So the “bean per teaspoon” ratio can be even lower than home versions while still meeting legal standards. ### 5. The jar looks generous, but the math is ruthless The illusion is that we see a jar stuffed with pods and think, “That’s a lot of vanilla.” And it *is* — relative to the vodka volume. But the flavor is so powerful that a single bean can perfume a huge amount of food. When you spread that one bean across 48 teaspoons of extract, and then spread that teaspoon across a dozen cookies, the bean-per-bite becomes almost homeopathic. That’s exactly why real vanilla is so expensive: you need to buy those beans, but you use them in such minute quantities per serving that the cost per cookie is still just a few cents. So yes, the home jar looks like you’re using a “substantial” amount of bean, but that’s only in the extraction vessel. In the final dish, it’s still just a whisper — a whisper from a very loud, bean-packed jar. That’s the paradox: vanilla is both precious and ethereal, and “vanilla flavored” labels honestly reflect that whisper’s source without ever promising physical heft.
